---
// Chapter III. Samwell, presented as a transcript. The check-in below is staged
// demo data from the Android store screenshots (open-citadel store-screenshots,
// persona "Marcus"), lightly shortened. It is not a real user's conversation,
// and the note under it says so.
import checkin from "../assets/screenshots/light/06-checkin.webp";

const shotAlt =
  "The same check-in in the app, with Samwell quoting a highlight from Leonardo's notebooks";
---

<section class="chapter samwell" id="samwell">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="chapter-mark">
      <span class="chapter-num">III</span>
      <span class="rule"></span>
      <span class="label label-dim">Your Self-Development Companion</span>
    </div>

    <div class="samwell-grid">
      <div>
        <div class="samwell-icon">
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.5"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <path
              d="m12 3-1.912 5.813a2 2 0 0 1-1.275 1.275L3 12l5.813 1.912a2 2 0 0 1 1.275 1.275L12 21l1.912-5.813a2 2 0 0 1 1.275-1.275L21 12l-5.813-1.912a2 2 0 0 1-1.275-1.275L12 3Z"
            ></path>
          </svg>
        </div>

        <h2 class="reveal">Turn what you learn into who you become.</h2>

        <p class="body">
          Samwell does three things. He talks through your books with you. He
          turns what you want into a goal you can keep. And he checks in using
          what you have logged and what you wrote.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          He can see your highlights, notes, and logs, so when a week goes badly
          he can show you what actually happened, in your own words.
        </p>

        <div class="transcript">
          <div class="turn ask">
            <span class="who">You</span>
            <p>
              Be honest with me. Marathon training is going great but the
              prototype feels stuck. What's going on?
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn reply">
            <span class="who">Samwell</span>
            <p>
              Honestly: your side goal is ahead of your main one. The marathon is
              93% consistent. The kit is 85% consistent, at 89 of 250 hours. At
              an even pace you'd be near 114 by now.
            </p>
            <p>The misses aren't random. Look at your last three Mondays:</p>
            <ol>
              <li>missed, "Legs dead after 22 km. Couch."</li>
              <li>missed, "24 km yesterday. Nothing left."</li>
              <li>1 hour, "An hour. Ordered the torque sensor at least."</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
              Each one came the evening after your longest run of the week: 22,
              24, then 26 km. On Tuesdays, Thursdays and Saturdays you hit your 2
              hours on 25 of 28 days. This isn't a discipline problem. The plan
              asks for your hardest thinking on your most tired evening.
            </p>
            <p>You highlighted this in July:</p>
            <blockquote>
              <p>
                Experience never errs; it is only your judgments that err by
                promising themselves effects such as are not caused by your
                experiments.
              </p>
              <cite>The Notebooks of Leonardo da Vinci</cite>
            </blockquote>
            <p>
              Your logs are the experiment. What got it wrong was the schedule,
              not you. You said it yourself on Sunday: "a plan written by someone
              who has never run 26 km."
            </p>
          </div>
          <p class="transcript-note">
            An example check-in. The person, numbers, and notes are made up.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="samwell-shot">
        <img
          src={checkin.src}
          width={checkin.width}
          height={checkin.height}
          alt={shotAlt}
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
